1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Knight Fjällar Pik=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Åke Pik and Kristina Giedda
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Notes'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Fjällnar Pik, 1364-1374, in which latter year he is mentioned as a knight. In 1364 he was one of King Magnus's representatives in the negotiations with the lords of Holstein and the Hanseatic cities and in 1371 he stood on King Hå
2 CONC kan's side at the treaty at Edsviken. He probably lived in Södermanland. -- He carried a mussel in his seal. His children were probably Sven Pik and Ingeborg Fjällarsdotter. ...Fjällarsdotter, married to Orm Folkesson (Gumshufvud)
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: PHT 1909, genealogical notes by K.H.Karlsson
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sok.riksarkivet.se/?Sokord=+Fj%C3%A4llar+Pik+&EndastDigitaliserat=false&AvanceradSok=False&page=1&postid=sdhk_7827&tab=post SDHK no: 7827]
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Dating: 1360 August 13''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl Erengisle Suneson, knights Magnus Nilsson and '''Fjällar Pik''' and Gotskalk Skarpenberg, on behalf of the Swedish and Norwegian kings, give permission for Duke Albrekt of Mecklenburg and his retinue to come to them at Rögnab
2 CONC ro and then return to Helsingborg. The layover is valid until sunset next Marie's ascension (15/8).
2 CONT Earl Erengisle Suneson seals.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Sources:''' Original:  Or. perg., Verträge mit Schweden no. 20, Mecklenburgisches Landeshauptarchiv, Schwerin
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sok.riksarkivet.se/?Sokord=+Fj%C3%A4llar+Pik+&EndastDigitaliserat=false&AvanceradSok=False&page=2&postid=sdhk_11415&tab=post SDHK no: 11415]
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Dating: 1379 January 18, Place of issue: Örebro''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT Erengisle Sunesson, earl of the Orkney Islands, Bo Jonsson, lawman of Drots and the (East) Goats, Karl Ulfsson, lawman of the Swedes (Upland), Vicke van Vitzen, bailiff in Kalmar, Sten Bengtsson, marshal of the realm, Bengt Filips
2 CONC son, Birger Ulfsson, Arvid Gustavsson, Bo Bosson, Anund Jonsson, Staffan Ulfsson, Sten Stensson, Knut Karlsson, Lars Björnsson, Folke Nilsson, the councillors, as well as Magnus Håkansson, (Peter) Porse Get, Nils Jonsson (or Jönss
2 CONC on, see the comment below), Birger Turesson, Ragvald Filipsson, '''Fjällar Pik''', Peter (Petersson) Porse, Bengt Bogh, Ulf Jonsson, Magnus Porse, Sten Bosson, Karl Magnusson, Ture Bengtsson, Arvid Bengtsson, Lars Ingebjörnsson, R
2 CONC agvald Nilsson, Håkan Fadersson, Staffan Nilsson, Harald Andersson, Torkel Haraldsson and Nils Arvidsson, saviors in (Sweden's) kingdom , petitions Pope Urban VI and the College of Cardinals regarding the canonization of Birgitta 
2 CONC (Birgersdotter). Christ has allowed the now deceased Birgitta to be born in the noble people of the Goths. She was a true and good person, eminent in conduct and reputation, and endowed with humble patience and other virtues, wher
2 CONC eby she enticed others from sins and errors. Birgitta benefited from divine visions and addresses, she revealed divine counsels and judgments, and the publishers believe that one must not conceal the great proof of her sanctity. A
2 CONC s early as thirty years ago, before she set out on a pilgrimage from the land of the issuers to Rome, she foretold by a revelation from Christ all the calamities which divine justice had afterwards brought upon the kingdom, and sh
2 CONC e also caused this to be said publicly as clearly and distinctly as if she had seen these misfortunes recorded in a book. These calamities still strike the kingdom with overwhelming force. Birgitta longed to be embraced by Christ 
2 CONC and deserved to be betrothed and called a bride by him. When she lived she shone upon the world as a star of virtue, and after her death she shines through the radiance of many signs. Therefore, a large number of people from diffe
2 CONC rent kingdoms, countries and with different languages ​​flock to Vadstena Kloster in the Diocese of Linköping, where her body is kept, and praise God for the miracles and omens that he performs daily through her. The Pope is there
2 CONC fore asked to enter Mrs. Birgitta in the catalog of saints and to canonize her and to order that she be venerated as a saint by the faithful and praised with due reverence. A petition in the same matter has previously been entered
2 CONC  by the issuers to the late representative of the Pope, and this renewed petition is now entered in unison when the issuers are assembled; the issues also include the councilors and saviors who are absent. The issuers seal.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Sources:''' Medieval transcripts:  [A] in hs A 14, fol. 239v–240r, Royal the library, Stockholm; [B] in Vadstena monastery's large copy book, hs A 20, fol. 307v–308r (without salutatio and dating phrase), Sv. The National Arc
2 CONC hives (image time ANSWER) ; [C] in Cod. Ottob. lazy. 90, fol. 132v–133r, Vatican Library.
2 CONT
2 CONT Literature and commentary
2 CONT On the mentioned members of the council of the kingdom see SRM (for Vicke van Vitzen see under Fikke van Vitzen).
2 CONT Several of the other saviors would later become council members. About the knight Magnus Håkansson (Magnus Marinason's family) see ÄSF I, p. 174; about the knight Birger Turesson (Boat) see L.-O. Larsson, Medieval Värend (1964), p
2 CONC . 413–415; about the knight Ragvald Filipsson (Lindöätten) see ÄSF I, p. 14, and JE Almquist, Law sagas and judgment sagas in Sweden I (1954), p. 143; about the knight Fjällar Pik see KH Karlsson in PHT 1909, p. 18; about the knig
2 CONC ht Peter (Petersson) Porse (three sea leaves) see A. Mohlin in PHT 1957, p. 72–73; about the squire Ulf Jonsson (Aspenäsätten), who was later dubbed a knight, see SRM p. 81; about the gunsmith Magnus Porse, see AF ​​Liljeholm in P
2 CONC HT 1955, p. 133, and ÄSF I, p. 54; about the squire Sten Bosson (Night and Day), who later became a knight, see SRM p. 73–74; about the squire, later the knight, Karl Magnusson (Eaglefoot) see SRM p. 51; about the squire, later th
2 CONC e knight, Ture Bengtsson (Bielke) see SRM p. 79; about the armorer Arvid Bengtsson (Lejonasikte, Bengt Nilsson's family) see ÄSF I, p. 73; about the gunsmith Lars Ingebjörnsson (Ekeblad from Västerfärnebo) see B. Hildebrand in SBL
2 CONC  XII, p. 612, and B. Fritz, Hus, land och län 1 (1972), p. 104; about the gunsmith Ragvald Nilsson (Fargalt) see ÄSF I, p. 30; about the gunner Håkan Fadersson (Stjärnbåt) see Almquist, aa, p. 311; on Staffan Nilsson Stangenberg s
2 CONC ee S. Kraft, Linköping's historia 1 (2nd ed. 1975), p. 212–213, and G. Tagesson, Bishop and city – aspects of urbanization and social spaces in medieval Linköping (2002), p. 404; about the armorer Harald Andersson (Bagge, three li
2 CONC lies) see H. Rosman, Bjärka-Säby I (1923), p. 92 f., and Almquist, aa, p. 285; about the squire, later the knight, Torkel Haraldsson (Gren) see ÄSF I, p. 57–58; about Nils Arvidsson (chess) see Almquist, aa, p. 290. The identifica
2 CONC tion of Nils Jonsson/Jönsson is uncertain. Possibly Nils Jönsson (Black Skåning) is meant, who, however, is said to appear in the sources no earlier than 1381; compare ÄSF I, p. 261. Bengt Bogh is probably to be identified with th
2 CONC e knight of this name who appears in letter 1383 14/4 (SDHK no. 12392 ) and in letter 1396 5/4 (SDHK no. 14578 ). For other comments see DS no. 9902 / SDHK no. 11413 .
2 CONT
