2 DATE BEF 1442
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Ingeborg Bengtsdotter (Vinstorp)=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Daughter of Bengt Uddsson (Vinstorp) and Kristina Staffansdotter (Ulv).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sok.riksarkivet.se/sbl/Presentation.aspx?id=13301 Gumsehuvud, genus, by  Swedish Biographical Dictionary]
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Volume 17 (1967-1969), page 414.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Gumsehuvud, now accepted name for a medieval family of freedmen, whose members themselves only used their first and middle names.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family probably descended from Folke Ormsson (d. c. 1359), who became a knight no later than 1343. He owned estates in Västergötland and carried a forked point in his coat of arms.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Gumsehuvud coat of arms can be attested within this family earliest (Gillingstam 1960) with Orm Folkesson (lived 1379). In 1373 he was imprisoned at Axvall in Västergötland, which was then held by King Håkan's supporters, and 
2 CONC must therefore have belonged to King Albrekt's party. The naming system and historical evidence of the estate indicate that he was the son of Folke Ormsson. A more hypothetical assumption is that he was married (Lundholm) into the
2 CONC  Sture family that carried the Gumsehuvud coat of arms, and that he adopted their coat of arms.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of Orm Folkesson's sons is called Fjällar Pik or Fjällar Ormsson (lived 1426) and was apparently named after a Södermanland knight Fjällar Pik, who was either his grandfather (Erslev; Karlsson) or his mother's previous husband
2 CONC  (Lundholm).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Another son of Orm Folkesson was Karl Ormsson (lived 1443). He gained entry into the high priesthood by marrying first the Södermanland lawyer Nils Erengislesson's ( Hammerstaätten ) sister's daughter Märta Gregersdtr ( Aspenäs
2 CONC ätten ) and then the councilor Staffan Ulfsson's (Ulv) daughter Ingeborg Bengtsdtr (Vinstorpaätten).''' As a councilor, he can certainly be attested first in the charter of privilege for the burghers of Vadstena that has been show
2 CONC n to originate from the meeting in Arboga in January 1435. However, he had already participated in councils of nobles in 1429, 1430, 1433 and 1434, most of whose participants were demonstrably councilors. Nils Jönsson (Oxenstierna
2 CONC ), who became councilor no later than 1432, appears both immediately before and immediately after him in the lists of names in the councilor letters, which may indicate that they became councilors at the same time. KO's attitude t
2 CONC owards King Erik is evident from the fact that at the meeting in Vadstena in August 1433, he, together with councilor Sture Algotsson, witnessed the protest of Bishop Tomas and two other Swedish bishops against Erik's having force
2 CONC d them to seal a letter to the Pope the previous month about the revocation of Olof Laurensson's appointment as archbishop of Uppsala. Two notaries attested that KO and Sture Algotsson had sworn before them that Erik had in their 
2 CONC presence induced the bishops to sign the seal with severe threats. On 5 October 1438, KO's daughter Katarina Karlsdtr (d. 1450) married Marshal Karl Knutsson (Bonde), who was appointed regent a few days later. In 1441 KO was a chi
2 CONC eftain at Västerås, and at King Christopher's coronation in 1441 he was knighted. In 1443 Christopher mentions him as his official at Axvall, where he is believed to have succeeded Bo Knutsson (Grip), who had been captured by King
2 CONC  Erik in 1442. In 1449 his third wife was a widow.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family died out with KO's son, the Upplandslagmann Gustav Karlsson (see below).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sources and literature'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources and litt: Perg: letter 18 Nov 1426, Genealogica 2, f 28, RA. — G Bendix, Hammerstaätten (PHT 1956); G Carlsson, Det engelbrektska upprorets begnynelskeskede (HT 1934), p 249 f; dens, Arboga möte 1435 (HT 1936), p 21, 26; d
2 CONC ens, Karl Knutssons testamente (HT 1943), p 5 f; DS 5 (1858—65); K Erslev, Slaegtskabsforbindeler mellem dansk og sv Adel i Tiden for Kalmarunionen (Personalhist Tidsskr 1:3, 1882); I Fredriksson, Sv perconnamnsstudier (1961), p 5
2 CONC 7, 169; H Gillingstam, Ätterna Oxenstierna o Vasa under medelt (1952—53); dens, Could one take up arms from one's wife's family during the Middle Ages? (PHT 1960); KH Karlsson, Medieval families with the name Pik (PHT 1909); K Kum
2 CONC lien, Karl Knutsson's political activities 1434—48 (1933); dens, Karl Ormsson (SMoK 4, 1948); I Lundahl, Det medelt Västergötland (1961); KG Lundholm, Gumsehuvud (ÄSF 1:1, 1957); dens, Drottning Katarina Karlsdtrs förfäder (PHT 19
2 CONC 58); Processus seu negocium canonizacionis b Ka-terine de Vadstenis (1942—46); SMR (1937); GT Westin, Karl Ormsson (Sv uppslagsbok, 15, 2 ed., 1950).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Refer to this article'''
2 CONT It is best, of course, if you can make a reference to the printed version. If you want to refer to the web version, you must make a link to the current page so that it is clear that it is the web version you are referring to. An e
2 CONC xample of a reference to this article is:
2 CONT Gumsehuvud, family, https://sok.riksarkivet.se/sbl/artikel/13301, Swedish Biographical Dictionary, retrieved 2026-05-24.You can also refer to this article using its unique URN number, which is: urn:sbl:13301 URN stands for Uniform
2 CONC  Resource Name and is a logical identifier for this article, unlike its link, which is a physical identifier. This means that a reference to the article's URN will always be valid, regardless of future changes to this web page. Su
2 CONC ch a reference might look like this: Gumsehuvud, släkt, urn:sbl:13301, Svenskt biografiskt lexikon, hämtad 2026-05-24.
