1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Cecilia Månsdotter (Ekaätten)=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Daughter of Magnus Karlsson Ekaätten & Sigrid Eskilsdotter Banér 
2 CONT *Birth: 1475  in Eka, Lillkyrka, Uppland, Sweden
2 CONT *Death:  1522 (43-51) the Blue Tower (Copenhagen Castle)
2 CONT * Cecilia Månsdotter ( Eka ), born after 1475, dead 1522 in the plague under captivity in the Blue Tower (Copenhagen Castle) , was a Swedish nobility lady. She is mainly known for being the mother of the Swedish king Gustav Vasa .
2 CONT * She was the daughter of Sigrid Eskilsdotter (Banér) and Måns Karlsson (Eka) . Her mother later remarried to Nils Eriksson (Gyllenstierna) , and their daughter Kristina Nilsdotter (Gyllenstierna) (married to the head of state Ste
2 CONC n Sture the younger ), was Cecilia's half-sister. Cecilia married Erik Johansson (Vaasa) (born around 1465, executed during the Stockholm massacre in 1520) and had eight children with him.
2 CONT * Cecilia became a widow when her husband was executed during the Stockholm massacre in 1520. She was imprisoned with her daughters, but was promised freedom by King Christian II if she persuaded her son Gustav to submit to him. S
2 CONC he accepted the proposal and wrote to him and tried to persuade him, but failed. In the spring of 1521, she was taken with her daughters and her mother to Copenhagen, where she was imprisoned along with a number of other Swedish n
2 CONC oblewomen. According to legend, she was murdered by King Kristian in revenge for her son conquering the Swedish throne, by forcing a sack, which she was then drowned in by being sewn into it and thrown into the sea. In reality, ho
2 CONC wever, she is believed to have died of the plague.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Married=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Married: [https://www.adelsvapen.com/genealogi/Vasa '''Erik Johansson Vasa,'''] Erik Johansson Vasa acted at Stockholm's bloodbath 1520-11-08. Heardshövding in Danderyd and Rydbo shipwreck. Knight and counselor. He was, as a per
2 CONC son, quite intimidated, arrogant, opposed to conflict, harsh and, moreover, very violent. Appealed to court several times for committing crimes. One of the crimes was theft in Ekerö church building, another was the murder of a man
2 CONC  who illegally committed an intrusion on Eriks farm. Married about 1492-95 with Cecilia Magnusdotter , daughter of Magnus Karlsson (Ekaätten) and Sigrid Eskilsdotter Banér . The canine Cecilia and her daughters died in Danish capt
2 CONC ivity in 1522.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Children=
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Birgitta Eriksdotter,''' † young.
2 CONT * '''Gustav I,''' † 1560, King of Sweden. The governor of Sweden for the years 1521-08-13 to 1523-06-06. King of Sweden from 1523-06-06 to 1560-09-29. Married 1: o 1531-09-24 in Stockholm with Princess Katarina of Saxony-Lauenburg
2 CONC  . Daughter of Duke Magnus I of Saxony-Lauenburg and Catherine of Braunschweig-Wolfenbüttel . Married 2: o 1536-10-01 in Stockholm with Margareta Eriksdotter Leijonhufvud , daughter daughter of the Swedish National Council and nat
2 CONC ional marshal Erik Abrahamsson Leijonhufvud and Ebba Eriksdotter Vasa . Married 3: o 1552-08-22in Vadstena Castle's wedding hall with Countess Katarina Stenbock , daughter of the Reverend Countess Gustaf Olofsson Stenbock and Libe
2 CONC rian Birgitta Eriksdotter Leijonhufvud .
2 CONT * '''Margareta Eriksdotter,''' † 1536-12-31 in Reval. Married 1: o 1516-03-30 at Stockholm Castle with Joachim Brahe . Married 2: o 1525-01-15 in Stockholm with the council, Count Johan by Hoya .
2 CONT *'''Måns,''' dead young.
2 CONT * '''Märta Eriksdotter,''' † in Copenhagen around 1522.
2 CONT * '''Johan,''' dead young.
2 CONT * '''Emerentia Eriksdotter,''' † in Danish captivity around 1522.  died of the plague in captivity in the Blue Tower in Copenhagen
2 CONT * '''Anna Eriksdotter,''' † Young in Vadstena Monastery.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source:''' [https://www.adelsvapen.com/genealogi/Vasa Vasa Geneology-Adelsvapen tab 7]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Cecilia Månsdotter Eka Biography=
2 CONT
2 CONT Cecilia was born around 1476 in Eka, Lillkyrka, which is now known as Eka, Örebro County in Sweden. She was the oldest of the two children from the nobles Sigrid Eskilsdotter Banér and Magnus Karlsson Eka. Her mother later remarri
2 CONC ed, and became the mother of the famous Christina Gyllenstierna in her second marriage, who was thereby Cecilia's half sister. Cecilia Månsdotter married the noble Erik Johansson Vasa before 1495 and with him had eight children.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1520, Cecilia's half sister Christina defended Stockholm from the Danish invasion, but was forced to surrender. Cecilia became a widow when her husband was executed at the Stockholm bloodbath in 1520. She, as well as her half s
2 CONC ister Christina Gyllenstierna, her mother and her daughters, belonged to the Swedish noblewomen taken as prisoners by the Danes. They were taken to Denmark in 1521 and imprisoned in the infamous Blaatornet (Blue Tower) in Copenhag
2 CONC en Castle - where she died of the plague in 1523, along with her two younger daughters Martha and Emerentia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cecilia was promised her freedom by King Christian, if she convinced her son Gustav to submit to him. She agreed and did enter in negotiations with her son and wrote to him, but failed to convince him.[1] Cecilia died the same yea
2 CONC r her son Gustav became king of the newly independent Sweden, which he had liberated from Denmark.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to legend, she was in fact executed by the King of Denmark. As an act of revenge after her son proclaimed himself King of Sweden, the King of Denmark forced her to sew a sack. After she was finished, he allegedly had her
2 CONC  placed in the sack and have it thrown in the sea, where she drowned.[2] There is no confirmation that this legend is true, however, though the women were said to have been badly treated in the prison by the cold air, harsh treatm
2 CONC ent and starvation. The way of execution was the same method by which her mother was nearly executed during the Stockholm Bloodbath. In reality, it is believed that she died of the plague along with her two youngest daughters.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Her mother, her half sister Christina, and her eldest daughter Margareta were eventually released and returned to Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[http://runeberg.org/sqvinnor/0105.html Cecilia to Eka, by Project Runeberg]
2 CONT
2 CONT Cecilia to Eka. This woman, so much mentioned in the history of our fatherland , was the mother of Gustaf I and died, as is usually said, in the awful Blue Tower prison in Copenhagen, of cold, hunger, neglect and cruel company, sh
2 CONC aring this fate with two of his daughters, as well as many of his closest female relatives and friends. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Cecilia was the daughter of Councilor Måns Carlsson to Eka and his wife Sigrid Banér and at a very young age married Mr. Erik Johansson Wase, who then, like so many nobles, fell victim to Christian tyranny at the so-called St
2 CONC ockholm massacre . Shortly afterwards a large number of the beheaded widows and children were taken to Copenhagen and thrown into prison. 
2 CONT
2 CONT It is even claimed by several authors that Cecilia, Gustaf Wasa's mother, would not have died in prison, but was condemned by Christian to be drowned, because he thus wished to take special revenge on the mother the much annoyed s
2 CONC on did to him; and it is said that she herself must sew the sack in which she was to be stopped, in order to be thrown into the lake. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She was not allowed to experience the victory of her noble son and the salvation of the motherland through him.  With her husband she had the following children: Gustaf, later King Gustaf I, Johan , dead young, Märtha and Emerenti
2 CONC a , twins, born 1472, died in the Blue Tower, Anna , nun in Wadstena monastery, Brita , died as a child, Magnus to  Rydboholm, died unmarried 1529, and Margaretha , first married to Joachim Brahe and then to Count Johan af Hoja. 
2 CONT
