1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://groups.google.com/g/soc.genealogy.medieval/c/ctSFaqrFIQQ/m/n_5BkDf-GeIJ
2 CONT SIR HUGH COKESEY died in 1445.
2 CONT His widow, Alice, married (2nd) (as his 2nd wife) Andrew Ogard, Knt.,
2 CONT of Buckenham, Norfolk, and Rye (in Stanstead Abbots), Hertfordshire,
2 CONT King's knight, Captain of Vire and Caen, Chamberlain and Councillor to
2 CONT John, Duke of Bedford, Constable of Prudhoe Castle, Ambassador to
2 CONT France, Knight of the Shire for Norfolk, 1453-1454, son of Peder
2 CONT Nielson, of Aagaard, Denmark. He was born in Denmark, and received
2 CONT letters of denization in England in 1436. They had one son, Henry,
2 CONT and one daughter, Anne (wife of Remfrey Arundel and Robert Crane,
2 CONT Esq.). Sir Andrew Ogard died shortly before 17 October 1454. He left
2 CONT a will requesting burial in the north side of the high altar in St.
2 CONT Mary's monastery in Wymondham, Norfolk. His widow, Alice, presented
2 CONT to the church at Attleborough, Norfolk in 1456. In the period, 1456-
2 CONT 1460, his widow, Alice, and the executors of his will including John
2 CONT Bourghchier, Knt., Lord Berners, were sued in Chancery by William
2 CONT Oldhall, Knt., regarding a payment under a statute of the staple at
2 CONT Westminster. His widow, Alice, died in 1460. Lysons, Magna Britannia
2 CONT 5 (1817): 129-142. Coll. Top. et Gen. 3 (1836): 105 (will of Andrew
2 CONT Ogard);
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.history.org.uk/events/calendar/9734/a-danish-knight-in-fifteenth-century-england-sir?srsltid=AfmBOoqXQ8FQTPM8Zx9EOTVa0s8j8QPeD5C-mJ6ePABRwIHFaT9T_3wY
2 CONT A Danish Knight in Fifteenth Century England; Sir Andrew Ogard
2 CONT Sir Andrew Ogard stands as a compelling, if, today, a lesser-known figure in the tapestry of late medieval England. From the 1420s to the 1440s, he forged for himself a prodigious martial reputation and amassed enormous wealth, se
2 CONC rving in Henry VI’s armies in France and Normandy during the Hundred Years’ War, where he was closely associated with the Dukes of Bedford and York. After retiring from military service, he played a significant role as a member of
2 CONC  the English gentry, in Norfolk and Hertfordshire. It is therefore perhaps surprising to discover that he was not English at all, having been born at Ågård in Denmark, as Anders Petersen Gyldenstjerne. A younger son of a noble Dan
2 CONC ish family from North Jutland, he had few prospects there, thus he left his native land to make his mark elsewhere. In so doing, he anglicised his name, also adopting a new surname based on his birthplace. Marrying into a prominen
2 CONC t and wealthy knightly Norfolk family, he became a leading member of the gentry, building two fortified manors at enormous expense, one at The Rye in Hertfordshire, the other at Emneth in Norfolk. He served the crown in many admin
2 CONC istrative capacities, on numerous royal commissions, and became a Knight of the Shire for Norfolk in the Parliament of 1453-4. As Patron of Wymondham Priory, he used his influence with the king and Pope to have the priory elevated
2 CONC  to abbey status. On his death in 1454, he was buried in the abbey, but his tomb is now lost, having been destroyed during the Dissolution of the Monasteries.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/184911786/andrew-ogard
2 CONT
2 CONT =============
2 CONT
2 CONT served the Duke of Bedford when he fought in France as regent in the minor of Henry VI (1413), and was his "second chamberlain", 1425 participates in a train towards Anjou, 1433-34 commanding officer of the fortress of Vire in Nor
2 CONC mandy, and was 1433 naturalized by the parliament (Henry VII), applied in 1445 the King of England to take possession of a him in Denmark fortified inheritance of tubular and real estate; sounded in England the name Sir Andrew Oga
2 CONC rde (Aagaard), lived 1454, well dead 1455. 
2 CONT Unmentioned letter writers, his relatives, suggested the 1458 widow after the "the one throughout England and several kingdoms acquaintance Andreas Agardis", According to his last will, a marriage between one of their daughters wh
2 CONC en the daughter grew up, and a son of Mr Axel Pedersson (Thott), to prevent the dispersion of her children's Paternity, as the children's paterts and also Mr Peder Eriksen, whom the widow had inserted for their guardians , had die
2 CONC d and therefore asked her to negotiate with the messenger Mr Peder Friis, Kannik in Ribe, and advised to send her daughter over here in time so that she could learn the country's seats and language.  1460 the children of Mr Anders
2 CONC  Pedersen are mentioned in Erik Eriksen Løvenbalks and his brother's change either in Aagaard or in the reedy goods with their maternal son Mourids Nielsen after their mother's mother, Miss. Karin. Jvfr. Will. Christensen, Missive
2 CONC r II 7 ff
2 CONT
2 CONT About Mr. Anders' Postage Haggard of Bradenham. Bokenham Castle, County Norfolk, which leads a six-odd silver star, see Pedigree from David Haggard (1530) in B. Burke, History of the gentry, I 1898, 647 f.
2 CONT
2 CONT tjente hertugen af Bedford, da denne kæmpede i Frankrig som regent i Henrik VI.s mindreårighed (1413), og var hans „second chamberlain", 1425 deltager i et tog mod Anjou, 1433-34 befalingsmand på fæstningen Vire i Normandiet, og b
2 CONC lev 1433 naturaliseret af parlamentet (Henrik VII), ansøgte 1445 kongen af England om at tage en ham i Danmark tilfalden arv af rørlig og fast ejendom i besiddelse; lød i England navnet Sir Andrew Ogarde (Aagaard), levede 1454, ve
2 CONC l død 1455.  Unævnte brevskrivere, hans slægtninge, foreslog 1458 enken efter "den i hele England og flere riger bekendte Andreas Agardis", i henhold til dennes sidste vilje, et giftermål mellem en af deres døtre, når datteren ble
2 CONC v voksen, og en søn af hr. Axel Pederssøn (Thott), for at forebygge, at hendes børns Fædrenearv adsplittedes, da børnenes farbrødre og også hr. Peder Eriksen, som enken havde indsat til deres formynder, var død, og anmodede hende 
2 CONC derfor om at forhandle med overbringeren hr. Peder Friis, kannik i Ribe, og tilrådede at sende datteren herover i tide, for at hun kunde lære landets sæder og sprog.  1460 nævnes hr. Anders Pedersens børn i Erik Eriksen Løvenbalks
2 CONC  og dennes broders skifte enten i Aagaard eller i rørligt gods med deres morbrodersøn Mourids Nielsen efter deres moders moder frk. Karin. Jvfr. Will. Christensen, Missiver II 7 ff.
2 CONT
2 CONT Om Hr. Anders' Efterslægt Haggard of Bradenham. Bokenham Castle, Grevskabet Norfolk, der fører en seksoddet Sølvstjerne, se Stamtavle fra David Haggard (1530) i B. Burke, History of the gentry, I 1898, 647 f.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://finnholbek.dk/getperson.php?personID=I44106&tree=2
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p22855.htm#i228548
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Andrew Ogard married Margaret Clifton, daughter of Sir John Clifton and Joan Thorpe.1 He died in 1459.1 He was buried at Wymondham, Norfolk, EnglandG.1
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Sir Andrew Ogard
2 CONT *Anne Ogard+2 She married, firstly, Renfrey/Remfrey Arundell, son of Sir Renfrey Arundell and Joan Colshull.1 She married, secondly, Robert Crane.1
2 CONT
